1

\begin{quote}
\textbf{1 Introduction}

We study treatment-effect estimation, using a panel of groups, indexed
by \emph{g}, observed at several time periods, indexed by \emph{t}.
\emph{Yg,t}, group \emph{g}'s period-\emph{t} outcome, may be affected
by \emph{Dg,t}, group \emph{g}'s period-\emph{t} treatment, but also by
\emph{g}'s lagged treatments.

In such instances, to estimate treatment effects, researchers often use
two-way fixed effects (TWFE) regressions of \emph{Yg,t} on group fixed
effects, period fixed effects, and some measures of contemporaneous and
past exposure to the treatment. A recent literature has shown that TWFE
regressions are not robust to heterogeneous treatment effects across
groups and/or over time. Another strand of literature has proposed
heterogeneity-robust difference-in-differences (DID) estimators, relying
on parallel-trends assumptions, like TWFE estimators, but robust to
heterogeneous treatment effects. In that second strand, most of the
papers that allow lagged treatments to affect the outcome assume that
treatment is binary and stag-gered, meaning absorbing. In this paper, we
propose heterogeneity-robust DID estimators, in applications where the
treatment is either non-binary and/or non-absorbing, and where the
lagged treatments may affect the outcome. Applications with a non-binary
and/or non-absorbing treatment are common. Of the 100 most-cited papers
published by the American Economic Review (AER) from 2015 to 2019, we
find that 26 estimate a TWFE regression, but only four have a
binary-and-absorbing treatment. Our estimators are widely applica-ble:
essentially, they can be used in any design where some groups keep their
period-one treatment for a few periods. They are computed by the
did\_multiplegt\_dyn Stata package (de Chaisemartin et al., 2023).1

Let \emph{Yg,t}(\emph{d}1\emph{, ..., dt}) denote the potential outcome
of \emph{g} at \emph{t}, if its treatments from period 1 to \emph{t} are
equal to (\emph{d}1\emph{, ..., dt}). Let \emph{Fg} denote the first
period at which group \emph{g}'s treatment changes, and\\
\emph{δg,ℓ}=
\emph{E}(\emph{Yg,Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ−Yg,Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ}(\emph{Dg,}1\emph{,
..., Dg,}1))\\
1did\_multiplegt\_dyn supersedes the did\_multiplegt Stata package, to
estimate treatment effects al-lowing for effects of lagged treatments on
the outcome.
\end{quote}

