Accordingly, when we aggregate the DID\emph{g,ℓ}estimators, we multiply
by minus one the DID\emph{g,ℓ}for groups such that \emph{Dg,Fg
\textless{} Dg,}1. This finally yields a DID\emph{ℓ}estimator of the
effect of having been exposed to a weakly higher treatment dose for
\emph{ℓ}periods.

In general, lending a more ``structural'' interpretation to the
DID\emph{ℓ}estimators---beyond their reduced-form interpretation---can
be challenging as our estimation method accommodates a range of
potentially complicated designs. For instance, with three periods and
three groups such that (\emph{D}1\emph{,}1 = 0\emph{, D}1\emph{,}2 =
4\emph{, D}1\emph{,}3 = 0), (\emph{D}2\emph{,}1 = 0\emph{, D}2\emph{,}2
= 2\emph{, D}2\emph{,}3 = 3), and (\emph{D}3\emph{,}1 = 0\emph{,
D}3\emph{,}2 = 0\emph{, D}3\emph{,}3 = 0), DID2 estimates the average of
\emph{E}(\emph{Y}1\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 4\emph{,}
0)\emph{−Y}1\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 0\emph{,} 0)) and
\emph{E}(\emph{Y}2\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 2\emph{,}
3)\emph{−Y}2\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 0\emph{,} 0)), so DID2 does not estimate
by how much the outcome increases on average when the treatment
increases by a given amount for a given number of periods.

In light of these challenges, we propose three strategies for
interpretable estimates that may be of interest to researchers. First,
when the number of \emph{Fg −}1-to-\emph{Fg −}1 + \emph{ℓ}treatment
trajectories is low relative to the number of groups, one may estimate
trajectory-specific versions of DID\emph{ℓ}, which may be more easily
interpretable. Second, we propose to normalize our DID\emph{ℓ}estimators
by the average
of\emph{ℓk}=1\emph{\textbar Dg,Fg−}1+\emph{k−Dg,}1\emph{\textbar{}}, the
total treatment increments from \emph{Fg} to \emph{Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ}that
generate \emph{δg,ℓ}. We show that DID\emph{nℓ}, the normalized
DID\emph{ℓ}, is unbiased for a weighted average of the effect of the
current treatment and of its \emph{ℓ−}1 first lags on the outcome. We
also show that DID\emph{nℓ}can be used to test a null hypothesis which
is often of great interest, namely whether the current and lagged
treatments all have the same effect on the outcome. Third, we show that
a weighted sum across \emph{ℓ}of the DID\emph{ℓ}s is unbiased for a
parameter with a clear economic interpretation, which may be used to
conduct a cost-benefit analysis assessing if the treatment changes that
took place over the panel led to a better situation than the one that
would have prevailed if no change had been undertaken, a natural policy
question. Importantly, that parameter can also be interpreted as an
average total effect per unit of treatment, where ``total effect''
refers to the sum of the effects of a treatment increment, at the time
when it takes place and at later periods.

4

Finally, we derive the asymptotic distribution of our estimators, and
confidence intervals for our target parameters. Consistent with the
spirit of our paper, those results are derived under weak conditions on
the design.

In our aforementioned census of highly-cited TWFE papers published in
the AER, papers that do not have a binary-and-staggered treatment and
estimate dynamic effects do so us-ing three different methods. In some
papers, there is no variation in treatment timing: all treated groups
start getting treated at the same date, with group-specific treatment
intensi-ties. Then, researchers have estimated TWFE regressions of the
outcome on the treatment intensity interacted with period fixed effects.
In more complicated designs where there may be variation in treatment
timing and a group's treatment may increase or decrease multiple times,
some researchers have estimated a panel-data version of the
local-projection method proposed by Jordà (2005), while other
researchers have estimated TWFE regressions of the outcome on the
treatment and its first \emph{K} lags, the so-called distributed-lag
regression. We show that under the parallel-trends assumption underlying
our estimators, those three re-gressions may not estimate a convex
combination of the \emph{δg,ℓ}s. Thus, they may not satisfy the no-sign
reversal property. The local-projection and distributed-lag regressions
also suffer from a contamination problem: coefficients supposed to
estimate the effect of a given length of exposure to treatment or of a
given treatment lag are actually contaminated by effects of other
exposure lengths or other lags. Strikingly, the local-projection
regression may yield biased estimators, possibly of the wrong sign, even
if the treatment effect is constant across groups and over time.
Therefore, despite their limitations, our estimators improve on current
practice.

We use our estimators to revisit Favara and Imbs (2015). These authors
use the differential timing and intensity of banking deregulations in US
states during the 1990s to estimate deregulations' effects on credit
supply. Our estimators suggest that banking deregulations have very
persistent effects on the growth of credit supply and house prices.
These findings differ sharply from those obtained by Favara and Imbs
(2015). Using the panel-data version of the local-projection method, the
authors find significant short-run effects, which quickly

5

\begin{quote}
vanish. Strikingly, our decompositions show that local-projection
estimators of the long-run effects of deregulations estimate weighted
sums of effects, where the sum of the weights is negative. Thus, even if
treatment effects are constant, those coefficients are of a different
sign than the effect.

The paper is organized as follows. Section 2 introduces the set up and
our assumptions. Section 3 defines our parameters of interest and
presents our estimators. Section 4 studies the properties of commonly
used TWFE regressions to estimate current and lagged treatment effects
in general designs. Section 5 uses our estimators to revisit Favara and
Imbs (2015). Finally, the Web Appendix details several extensions and
gathers all the proofs.

\textbf{Related literature}

Our paper extends previous literature on heterogeneity-robust DID
estimators, when lagged treatments can affect the outcome (see, e.g.,
Callaway and Sant'Anna, 2021; Sun and Abra-ham, 2021; Borusyak, Jaravel
and Spiess, 2023), because prior papers assume that treatment is binary
and absorbing, unlike this paper. Yet, there are cases, reviewed in
details at the end of Section 3.1, where after some relabelling, our
non-normalized estimators are numerically equivalent to some estimators
of Callaway and Sant'Anna (2021). Thus, the main contribu-tions we make
to that strand of literature amount to highlighting DID\emph{ℓ}'s lack
of ``structural''interpretation in the complex designs we consider, and
proposing three strategies to obtain more interpretable estimators.
\end{quote}

This paper is also related to our prior work in de Chaisemartin and
D'Haultfœuille (2020). There, we also considered potentially non-binary
and/or non-absorbing treatments, but we assumed that lagged treatments
do not affect the outcome. There are two differences between this paper
and our prior work. First, when lagged treatments can affect the outcome
and the treatment is non-binary and/or non-absorbing, the DIDM estimator
in de Chaisemartin and D'Haultfœuille (2020) may not unbiasedly estimate
the effect of the contemporaneous treat-ment on the outcome, unlike the
DID1 estimator in this paper.3Second, with a non-binary 3Consider a
binary treatment that may turn on an off. Assume that group \emph{g} is
untreated at period 1,

6

\begin{quote}
treatment, de Chaisemartin and D'Haultfœuille (2020) also consider a
normalized estimator, but the normalization in this paper is different.
de Chaisemartin and D'Haultfœuille (2020) normalize the effect of
switches by switchers' average treatment change, to ensure DIDM has a
slope interpretation. Here, because potential outcomes are a function of
several arguments (the current and the lagged treatments), the
normalization that leads to the ``weighted av-erage of the effects of
different treatment lags'' interpretation divides DID\emph{ℓ}by the sum
of switchers' treatment increments from \emph{Fg} to \emph{Fg} +
\emph{ℓ}.

In work posterior to ours, Callaway, Goodman-Bacon and Sant'Anna (2021)
consider designs where groups are all untreated at period one, and then
get treated at heterogeneous dates, with heterogeneous intensities. Our
estimators can also be used in those designs. Some of the estimands they
propose compare the outcome evolution of treated and not-yet-treated,
like ours.

An important literature has proposed estimators of the effects of
current and lagged treat-ments, when the treatment can both increase or
decrease over time, under a sequential ignorability assumption. This
assumption requires that at each period, the treatment is independent of
current or future potential outcomes, conditional on past treatments and
outcomes (see Robins, 1986; Murphy et al., 2001; Bojinov, Rambachan and
Shephard, 2021). Instead, we consider a parallel-trends assumption, and
consider alternative estimands and estimators under that assumption.

Finally, our decompositions of the regression coefficients used by
applied researchers to es-timate dynamic effects in general designs are
related to the literature showing that TWFE regressions are not robust
to heterogeneous treatment effects (see, e.g., de Chaisemartin and

untreated at period 2, and treated at period 3. Ruling out dynamic
effects, any group \emph{g′}that is untreated at periods 2 and 3 is a
valid control group under a parallel trends assumption on
\emph{Yg,t}(0). However, in that set of control groups, there may be
some groups that were treated at period 1. Allowing for dynamic effects,
such \emph{g′}become invalid control groups: their period-one treatment
may still affect their period-two-to-three outcome evolution. Therefore,
unlike DIDM, the DID1 estimator in this paper does not use those
\emph{g′}as control groups.
\end{quote}

7

\begin{quote}
D'Haultfœuille, 2020; Goodman-Bacon, 2021; Borusyak and Jaravel, 2017),
and in particular to Sun and Abraham (2021) and de Chaisemartin and
D'Haultfœuille (2023\uline{a}), who respec-tively study event-study
regressions with a binary and staggered treatment, and regressions with
several treatments. Our main contribution to this literature is our
decomposition of the TWFE local-projection regressions. The fact that
those regressions are misspecified and could exhibit sign reversal even
under constant effects is unique to that particular class of TWFE
regressions.

\textbf{2 Setup, design, and identifying assumptions}

\textbf{2.1} \textbf{Setup}

\textbf{Group-level panel data.} We have a panel of \emph{G} groups
observed at \emph{T} periods, respectively indexed by \emph{g} and
\emph{t}. Typically, groups are geographical entities, like states or
counties, but a group could also just be a single individual or firm.
The group-level panel data may be constructed by aggregating an
individual-level panel or repeated cross-section data set at the
(\emph{g, t}) level, defining groups, say, as individuals' county of
birth. The group-level panel data may also be constructed from a single
cross-section, with cohort of birth playing the role of time. The
estimators we propose below are not weighted by \emph{Ng,t}, the
population of cell (\emph{g, t}). This is just to reduce notational
complexity: proposing weighted estimators is a mechanical extension.4

\textbf{Treatment.} Let \emph{Dg,t} denote the treatment of group
\emph{g} at period \emph{t}. Throughout the paper, we assume that the
treatment is non-negative: \emph{Dg,t ≥}0, as is most often the case in
appli- 4The did\_multiplegt\_dyn command that implements the estimators
proposed in this paper can be used with data at a more disaggregated
level than the (\emph{g, t}) level. Then, it aggregates the data at the
(\emph{g, t}) level internally and automatically weights (\emph{g, t})
cells by their number of observations in the data. To use different
weights, or to weight the estimation when working directly with a
group-level panel data, one can use the weight option.
\end{quote}

8

\begin{quote}
cations.5Let \emph{\textbf{D}g} = (\emph{Dg,}1\emph{, ..., Dg,T}) be a
vector stacking \emph{g}'s treatments from period 1 to \emph{T}, and let
\emph{\textbf{D}} = (\emph{\textbf{D}}1\emph{, ..., \textbf{D}G}) be a
vector stacking the treatments of all groups at every period. We refer
to \emph{\textbf{D}} as the study's design. Finally, let \emph{D} be the
set of values \emph{\textbf{D}g} can take (i.e.: its support).

\textbf{Potential outcomes.} For all (\emph{d}1\emph{, ..., dT})
\emph{∈D}, let \emph{Yg,t}(\emph{d}1\emph{, ..., dT}) denote the
potential out-come of group \emph{g} at \emph{t} if (\emph{Dg,}1\emph{,
..., Dg,T}) = (\emph{d}1\emph{, ..., dT}), and let \emph{Yg,t} =
\emph{Yg,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g}) denote the ob-served outcome of \emph{g}
at \emph{t}. This dynamic potential outcome framework follows Robins
(1986).

It explicitly allows groups' outcome at \emph{t} to depend on their past
and future treatments.

\textbf{Initial conditions.} Some observations may have already been
treated prior to period 1, and those treatments may still affect some of
their period-1-to-\emph{T} outcomes, the so-called initial-conditions
problem. However, we cannot estimate those effects, as treatments and
outcomes are not observed before period 1, so we do not account for this
potential dependency in our notation. We discuss strategies to account
for this dependency in Section 1.8 of the Web Appendix.

\textbf{Conditioning on the design.} When defining our target
parameters, we take the per-spective of a social planner, seeking to
conduct a cost-benefit analysis comparing groups'actual treatments
\emph{\textbf{D}} to the counterfactual ``status-quo'' scenario where
every group would have kept all the time the same treatment as in period
1. Thus, the planner wants to know if the treatment/policy changes that
took place over the duration of the panel led to a better situation than
the one that would have prevailed without any policy change, a natural
policy question. Then, our analysis is conditional on \emph{\textbf{D}},
the study's design. This implies that our parameters of interest are
dictated by the design, rather than chosen by the researcher. As such,
they do not exhaust the set of all possible treatment effect parameters
one may be in-terested in, and only partially uncover the structural
function mapping the current, lagged, and future treatments to the
average outcome. Conditional on the design, only groups' po- 5When
\emph{Dg,t ≥\uline{d} \textless{}} 0, one can redefine the treatment as
\emph{Dg,t −\uline{d}}, a non-negative treatment.
\end{quote}

9

tential outcomes are random, and probabilistic statements below are with
respect to their joint probability distribution. We take a model-based
approach to uncertainty: potential outcomes are random because of
shocks.

\textbf{2.2} \textbf{Design}

\textbf{Condition that the design has to satisfy for our estimators to
be applicable.} The date at which a group's treatment changes for the
first time plays a key role in our analysis.

\textbf{Definition 1} \emph{(First treatment change) For all g, let Fg}
= min\emph{\{t} : \emph{t ≥}2\emph{, Dg,t ̸}= \emph{Dg,t−}1\emph{\}.}

We adopt the convention that \emph{Fg} = \emph{T} + 1 if \emph{g}'s
treatment never changes. Our estimators are applicable to any design
that satisfies Restriction 1 below.

\textbf{Design Restriction 1} \emph{∃}(\emph{g, g′}) \emph{such that:
(i) Dg,}1 = \emph{Dg′,}1\emph{, (ii) Fg ̸}= \emph{Fg′.}

(i) requires that there exist groups with the same period-one treatment.
This is essentially a restriction on the support of the period-one
treatment. If groups' period-one treatments are i.i.d. draws from a
continuous distribution, \emph{Dg,}1 \emph{̸}= \emph{Dg′,}1 for all
(\emph{g, g′}), so (i) fails. On the other hand, if \emph{Dg,}1 can only
take a finite number of values \emph{K}, (i) automatically holds as long
as \emph{K \textless{} G}. In Section 1.10 of our Web Appendix, we
extend our estimators to designs where (i) fails, but for expositional
purposes we prefer to assume throughout the paper that (i) holds. (ii)
requires that there is heterogeneity in the date at which groups change
treatment for the first time. (ii) fails if groups' treatment is
extremely non-persistent, so that \emph{Dg,}1 \emph{̸}= \emph{Dg,}2 and
\emph{Fg} = 2 for all \emph{g}. (ii) also fails if all groups keep their
period-one treatment throughout the panel: \emph{Fg} = \emph{T} + 1 for
all \emph{g}, or if groups all change treatment for the first time at
the same date, for instance due to a universal policy affecting them
all: \emph{Fg} = \emph{t}0 for all \emph{g}. Overall, though we impose
(i) in the paper to simplify exposition, the only designs where our
estimators are inapplicable are those where groups' treatment is
extremely non-persistent, and designs with a universal treatment change.

10

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Some commonly-found designs where Design Restriction 1 often
holds.}

\textbf{Design 1} \emph{(Binary and staggered treatment) ∀}(\emph{g,
t})\emph{, Dg,t} = 1\emph{\{t ≥Fg\}, with Fg ≥}2\emph{.} Design 1 has
been studied by Callaway and Sant'Anna (2021), Goodman-Bacon (2021), Sun
and Abraham (2021), and Borusyak, Jaravel and Spiess (2023), among
others. Assuming that all groups are initially untreated (\emph{Fg ≥}2)
is without loss of generality: in Design 1, the treatment effects of
always-treated groups cannot be estimated under parallel-trends
assumptions.

\textbf{Design 2} \emph{(Binary treatment, groups can join and then
leave treatment) ∀}(\emph{g, t})\emph{, Dg,t} = 1\emph{\{Eg ≥t ≥Fg\},
with} 2 \emph{≤Fg ≤Eg.}

In Design 2, groups may get treated and leave treatment. For instance,
the design of Burgess et al. (2015) is very close to Design 2.6An
important special case of Design 2 is when treated groups are treated
for only one period (\emph{Eg} = \emph{Fg}), leading to

\emph{Dg,t} = 1\emph{\{t} = \emph{Fg\},} (1)

which we hereafter refer to as a ``one-shot-treatment design''.

\textbf{Design 3} \emph{(Staggered design with group-specific
intensities) ∀}(\emph{g, t})\emph{, Dg,t} = \emph{Ig}1\emph{\{t ≥Fg\},
Fg ≥}2\emph{.}

In Design 3, there is variation across groups in treatment timing and/or
intensity. For instance, the design of Pierce and Schott (2016)
corresponds to Design 3.

\textbf{Design 4} \emph{(Zero treatment at baseline) ∀g, Dg,}1 =
0\emph{.}

Designs 1-3 are special cases of Design 4. We single out Design 4,
because such designs are frequent. Our estimators can be used outside of
that design, like in Design 5 below.

\textbf{Design 5} \emph{(Discrete treatment at baseline) ∀g, Dg,}1
\emph{∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{, ..., K\}.}

6Specifically, for the co-ethnicity \emph{×} democracry treatment in
their Equation (2), districts can enter and leave treatment at most
twice over their entire study period.
\end{quote}

11

In Design 5, groups' baseline treatment is discrete, and treatment paths
are unrestricted. For instance, the design of Fuest, Peichl and Siegloch
(2018) corresponds to Design 5. A special case of Design 5 is when
groups' baseline treatment is binary.

\textbf{2.3} \textbf{Identifying assumptions}

\textbf{No-anticipation.} We maintain throughout the paper the following
condition.

\textbf{Assumption 1} \emph{(No Anticipation) ∀g, ∀}(\emph{d}1\emph{,
..., dT}) \emph{∈D, Yg,t}(\emph{d}1\emph{, ..., dT}) =
\emph{Yg,t}(\emph{d}1\emph{, ..., dt})\emph{.}

Assumption 1 requires that a group's current outcome does not depend on
its future treat-ments, the so-called no-anticipation hypothesis. This
assumption appears in Robins (1986), Abbring and Van den Berg (2003),
Malani and Reif (2015), Botosaru and Gutierrez (2018), Callaway and
Sant'Anna (2021), and Sun and Abraham (2021).

\textbf{Parallel trends.} Let \emph{D}r 1= \emph{\{d} :
\emph{∃}(\emph{g, g′}) \emph{∈\{}1\emph{, ..., G\}}2 : \emph{Dg,}1=
\emph{Dg′,}1= \emph{d, Fg̸}= \emph{Fg′\}} be the set of
period-one-treatment values such that two groups with different values
of \emph{Fg} have that period-one treatment. For any \emph{d} in
\emph{D}r 1and any \emph{t}, let \emph{\textbf{d}t}denote a 1 \emph{× t}
vector of \emph{d}s. For any integer \emph{k}, let
\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,k} be a 1 \emph{× k} vector whose coordinates
are all equal to \emph{Dg,}1. \emph{Yg,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t})
is \emph{g}'s period-\emph{t} outcome in a counterfactual where it keeps
its period-one treatment till period \emph{t}. We refer to it as its
``status-quo'' potential outcome.

\textbf{Assumption 2} \emph{(Parallel trends for the status-quo outcome,
for groups with the same period-one treatment) ∀}(\emph{g, g′})\emph{,
if Dg,}1 = \emph{Dg′,}1 \emph{∈Dr} 1\emph{, then ∀t ≥}2\emph{,}

\emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}
= \emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg′,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg′,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}\emph{.}

Assumption 2 requires that if two groups have the same period-one
treatment, then they have the same expected evolution of their
status-quo outcome. Assumption 2 is a generalization of the standard
parallel-trends assumption in DID models (see, e.g., Abadie, 2005) to
our set-up that allows for dynamic effects and for potentially
complicated designs where groups may not all be untreated at period one.
In Design 4, under Restriction 1, \emph{D}r 1= \emph{\{}0\emph{\}}, so
Assumption 2 12

only restricts the never-treated potential outcome
\emph{Yg,t}(\textbf{0}\emph{t}), and is similar to the identifying
assumption considered by Callaway and Sant'Anna (2021) and Sun and
Abraham (2021) for binary and staggered designs. Note that Assumption 2
restricts only one potential outcome per group, so even when \emph{D}r
1contains more than one value, Assumption 2 alone does not restrict
groups' treatment effects. If Assumption 2 is implausible for some
values in \emph{D}r 1, the estimators we propose can still be used,
excluding groups with \emph{Dg,}1 equal to one of those values.
Assumption 2 has testable implications that are easy to assess, using
placebo estimators mimicking the actual estimators we propose (see
Section 3.5).

