the number and the recency of the treatment periods that gener-ate
\emph{δg,ℓ}vary across groups, complicating the interpretation of
\emph{δℓ}. Similarly, with three periods and three groups such that
(\emph{D}1\emph{,}1 = 0\emph{, D}1\emph{,}2 = 4\emph{, D}1\emph{,}3 =
0), (\emph{D}2\emph{,}1 = 0\emph{, D}2\emph{,}2 = 2\emph{, D}2\emph{,}3
= 3), and (\emph{D}2\emph{,}1 = 0\emph{, D}2\emph{,}2 = 0\emph{,
D}2\emph{,}3 = 0), DID2 estimates the average of
\emph{E}(\emph{Y}1\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 4\emph{,}
0)\emph{−Y}1\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 0\emph{,} 0)) and
\emph{E}(\emph{Y}2\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 2\emph{,} 3)
\emph{−Y}2\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 0\emph{,} 0)). Thus, the magnitude and
timing of the treatment increments generating \emph{δg,ℓ}varies across
groups, which again complicates the interpretation of \emph{δℓ}. Still,
in any design satisfying Restriction 2, \emph{δℓ}can be interpreted as
an average effect of having been exposed to a weakly higher treatment
for \emph{ℓ}periods.

