with the same path, thus yielding estimates of the average effects of
specific treatment paths.10 For instance, in Design 2 the number of
treatment paths may often be low enough for this solution to be
practical. But in more complicated designs where treatment paths are
very group-specific, \emph{Pℓ}may often be too large for this solution
to be practical, especially as \emph{ℓ}increases. In such instances, we
still recommend that practitioners report the values in
\emph{\{}(\emph{Dg,}1\emph{, Dg,Fg, ..., Dg,Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ}) : \emph{Fg
−}1 + \emph{ℓ≤Tg\}}, and their distribution: this information may be
helpful to interpret DID\emph{ℓ}.

\textbf{Comparison with unconditional event-study estimators of Callaway
and Sant'Anna (2021) in Design 4.} When all groups have the same
period-one treatment, DID\emph{ℓ}is equiv-alent to the estimator
obtained by redefining the treatment as an indicator equal to one if
group \emph{g}'s treatment has ever changed at \emph{t}, and then
computing the estimator of \emph{θes}(\emph{ℓ−}1) of Callaway and
Sant'Anna (2021) with this binarized and staggerized treatment.11

\textbf{Comparison with unconditional event-study estimators of Callaway
and Sant'Anna (2021) outside of Design 4.} When groups' period-one
treatment varies, the two estima-tors are not
equivalent:12DID\emph{ℓ}only compares switchers and non-switchers with
the same period-one treatment, whereas the aforementioned estimator
compares switchers and non-switchers with different period-one
treatments. Then, that estimator relies on Assumption 3:\\
