Deryugina (2017) or Krolikowski (2018).

12 There are recent papers that binarize and staggerize the treatment
and compute the unconditional event-study estimators of Callaway and
Sant'Anna (2021) outside of Design 4, see, e.g. East et al. (2023).
\end{quote}

17

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Assumption 3} \emph{(Parallel trends for the status-quo outcome,
for all groups) ∀}(\emph{g, g′})\emph{, ∀t ≥}2\emph{,}

\emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}
= \emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg′,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg′,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}\emph{.}

Assumption 3 is stronger than Assumption 2. To simplify the remainder of
the discussion, let us assume that treatment is binary. Then, combined
with the standard parallel-trends assumption on groups' never-treated
outcome, Assumption 3 implies that for all groups such that \emph{Dg,}1
= 1,

\emph{E} {[}\emph{Yg,t}(\textbf{1}\emph{t})
\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\textbf{1}\emph{t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]} =
\emph{E} {[}\emph{Yg,t}(\textbf{0}\emph{t})
\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\textbf{0}\emph{t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}
\emph{,}\\
\emph{⇐⇒E} {[}\emph{Yg,t}(\textbf{1}\emph{t})
\emph{−Yg,t}(\textbf{0}\emph{t})\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]} =
\emph{E} {[}\emph{Yg,t−}1(\textbf{1}\emph{t−}1)
\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\textbf{0}\emph{t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}
\emph{.} (3)

(3) means that in initially treated groups, the effect of being treated
for \emph{t} periods should be the same as the effect of being treated
for \emph{t −}1 periods. This is an unpalatable restric-tion: it fails
whenever lagged treatments affect the outcome and/or treatment effects
are time-varying. By contrast, when combined with the standard
parallel-trends assumption on groups' never-treated outcome, Assumption
2 implies that for all groups such that \emph{Dg,}1 = 1,

\emph{E}(\emph{Yg,t}(\textbf{1}\emph{t})
\emph{−Yg,t}(\textbf{0}\emph{t})\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}})
\emph{−}(\emph{E}(\emph{Yg,t−}1(\textbf{1}\emph{t−}1)
\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\textbf{0}\emph{t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}})) does
not vary across \emph{g.}

This means that the incremental effect of one treatment period should be
the same in every initially treated group. This is of course a strong
restriction, but unlike (3), it does not rule out effects of lagged
treatments on the outcome, and it allows for time-varying effects.13

\textbf{Comparison with some conditional event-study estimators of
Callaway and Sant'Anna (2021).} When groups' period-one treatment varies
and groups' treatment is always weakly larger than their baseline
treatment, DID\emph{ℓ}is equivalent to the estimator obtained by
com-puting the Callaway and Sant'Anna (2021) estimator with the
binarized and staggerized 13de Chaisemartin and D'Haultfœuille (2018)
and de Chaisemartin and D'Haultfœuille (2020) had already noted that in
a model assuming away effects of lagged treatments on the outcome,
parallel trends across groups with different baseline treatments
essentially rules out time-varying effects.
\end{quote}

18

\begin{quote}
treatment, controlling non-parametrically for groups' baseline
treatments. Despite this nu-merical equivalence, Callaway and Sant'Anna
(2021) consider binary and staggered treat-ments and do not suggest
controlling for groups' period-one treatment: highlighting the
importance of controlling for groups' baseline treatment is a
contribution of this paper. Fi-nally, when groups' period-one treatment
varies and groups can experience a strictly lower treatment than their
baseline treatment, DID\emph{ℓ}is not equivalent to a version of the
Callaway and Sant'Anna (2021) estimator.

