\textbf{Interpretation of} \emph{δ} \textbf{as an average total effect
per unit of treatment.} Let us first consider a simple example with two
groups and four periods, such that \emph{D}1\emph{,}1 = 0,
\emph{D}1\emph{,}2 = 1, \emph{D}1\emph{,}3 = 1, and \emph{D}1\emph{,}4 =
0, while group 2 is never treated. Then,

\begin{quote}
\emph{δ} =\emph{E} {[}\emph{Y}1\emph{,}2(0\emph{,} 1)
\emph{−Y}1\emph{,}2(0\emph{,} 0) + \emph{Y}1\emph{,}3(0\emph{,}
1\emph{,} 1) \emph{−Y}1\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 0\emph{,} 0) +
\emph{Y}1\emph{,}4(0\emph{,} 1\emph{,} 1\emph{,} 0)
\emph{−Y}1\emph{,}4(0\emph{,} 0\emph{,} 0\emph{,}
0)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]} 1 + 1 + 0\\
= 1 2\emph{E} {[}\emph{Y}1\emph{,}2(0\emph{,} 1)
\emph{−Y}1\emph{,}2(0\emph{,} 0) + \emph{Y}1\emph{,}3(0\emph{,}
1\emph{,} 0) \emph{−Y}1\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 0\emph{,} 0) +
\emph{Y}1\emph{,}4(0\emph{,} 1\emph{,} 0\emph{,} 0)
\emph{−Y}1\emph{,}4(0\emph{,} 0\emph{,} 0\emph{,}
0)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]} + 1 2\emph{E}
{[}\emph{Y}1\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 1\emph{,} 1)
\emph{−Y}1\emph{,}3(0\emph{,} 1\emph{,} 0) +
\emph{Y}1\emph{,}4(0\emph{,} 1\emph{,} 1\emph{,} 0)
\emph{−Y}1\emph{,}4(0\emph{,} 1\emph{,} 0\emph{,}
0)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]} \emph{.} (5)
\end{quote}

The first expectation in (5) is the total effect produced by group 1's
period-2 treatment, at periods 2, 3, and 4, relative to the situation
where it would have always remained untreated. The second expectation in
(5) is the total effect produced by group 1's period-3 treatment, at
periods 3 and 4, conditional on its period-2 treatment and relative to
the situation where it would have been untreated at periods 3 and 4.
Accordingly, \emph{δ} is the average \emph{total} effect of those two
treatments. A similar interpretation holds beyond this simple example.
