Assumption 4 allows for serial correlation of the treatments and
outcomes within each group, which are important features to account for
in DID studies (Bertrand, Duflo and Mul-lainathan, 2004). Assumption 4
is also weaker than the common assumption that (\emph{\textbf{D}g,
\textbf{Y}g})\emph{g≥}1 are i.i.d. or even simply independent. In
particular, it allows for any form of dependence between groups'
treatments (\emph{\textbf{D}g})\emph{g≥}1. Assumption 5 may be seen as a
reinforcement and an asymptotic version of Restriction 1. It is stated
as an assumption because it is not directly

25

verifiable from the data. Point 1 requires that the number of values of
the baseline treatment remains finite when the number of groups goes to
infinity. Point 2 requires that there is at least one value of
\emph{ℓ}for which the number of groups such that \emph{δg,ℓ}can be
estimated goes to \emph{∞}a.s., which is necessary to have that
DID\emph{ℓ}is consistent. With i.i.d. groups, \emph{Nℓ/G} converges
almost surely to a positive constant for all \emph{ℓ∈L}. Instead, we do
not impose any restriction on the rate at which \emph{Nℓ}goes to
infinity. Point 3 requires that if the number of groups \emph{g} such
that (i) \emph{δg,ℓ}can be estimated and (ii) (\emph{Dg,}1\emph{, Sg}) =
(\emph{d, s}) goes to infinity, then the number of groups \emph{g′}with
\emph{Dg′,}1 = \emph{d} that switch the latest (or that never switch)
also tends to infinity at the same rate. Otherwise, some control groups
intervening in DID\emph{ℓ}may have a weight tending to infinity, which
could make asymptotic normality of DID\emph{ℓ}fail. Noteworthy, Point 3
of Assumption 5 automatically holds if groups' treatment paths
(\emph{\textbf{D}g})\emph{g≥}1 are i.i.d. Finally, Assumption 6 ensures
in particular that we can apply the Lyapunov central limit theorem in
our set-up with independent but not identically distributed variables.
The second con-dition therein prevents degenerate situations where some
non-trivial linear combinations of the (\emph{Yg,}1\emph{, ..., Yg,T})
in DID\emph{ℓ}would actually be constant.

