Theorem 1 shows that for almost all realizations of
(\emph{\textbf{D}g})\emph{g≥}1, DID\emph{ℓ}is asymptotically normal,

and the confidence interval of \emph{δℓ}is asymptotically conservative.
Moreover, if groups are

identically distributed and \emph{\textbf{D}g} is a function of
(\emph{Dg,}1\emph{, Fg, Sg}), the confidence interval reaches

its nominal level asymptotically. This latter condition holds if the
treatment is binary and

can change only once, as is for instance the case in Design 1. Finally,
the results of Theorem 1

easily extend to the normalized parameter \emph{δnℓ}: recall that
\emph{δnℓ}= \emph{δℓ/δDℓ}and DID\emph{nℓ}= DID\emph{ℓ/δDℓ},

where \emph{δDℓ}is a function of \emph{\textbf{D}} only. Similar
asymptotic results could also be obtained for the

cost-benefit parameter \emph{δ}.

