provided those differential trends are fully explained by changes in
their covariates. Then, we estimate \emph{θd} by regressing \emph{Yg,t
−Yg,t−}1 on \emph{Xg,t −Xg,t−}1 in the sample of (\emph{g, t})s such
that \emph{Dg,}1 = \emph{d} and \emph{Fg \textgreater{} t}. Finally, we
