\textbf{4.2} \textbf{Local-projection panel regressions.}

\textbf{Local-projection panel regressions.} Among the nine papers in
our census that do not have a binary and staggered design and estimate
dynamic effects, the second estimation method we found are regressions
of leads of the outcome on group and period FEs and the treatment. Such
regressions have sometimes been described as a panel-data version of the
local-projection method originally proposed by Jordà (2005) for
time-series data.

\textbf{Regression 2} \emph{(Local-projection panel regressions) For
every ℓ∈\{}1\emph{, ..., T −}1\emph{\} such that the regression that
follows is well-defined, letβlp,ℓdenote the coefficient on Dg,t in a
regression of Yg,t−}1+\emph{ℓon group and period FEs and Dg,t, in the
subsample such that} 1 \emph{≤t ≤T −ℓ}+ 1\emph{.}

Intuitively, researchers hope that \emph{βlp,ℓ}estimates some average
effect of increasing groups'period-\emph{t} treatment by one unit on
their period \emph{t −}1 + \emph{ℓ}outcome. Researchers may estimate
Regression 2 in first-difference. A result similar to that in
Proposition 2 below also applies to that specification.

\textbf{A decomposition of the coefficients in local-projection panel
regressions.} We decompose local-projection panel regressions in Design
4: the papers in our census that have used those regressions fall into
that design. For all \emph{ℓ∈\{}1\emph{, ..., T −}1\emph{\}},
let\emph{εℓg,t}denote the residual from a regression of \emph{Dg,t} on
group and time fixed effects, in the subsample such that 1 \emph{≤t ≤T
−ℓ}+ 1. For all \emph{g} such that \emph{Fg ≤T} and all \emph{k
∈\{}1\emph{, ..., T −Fg} + 1\emph{\}}, let

