\end{longtable}

sum is across groups, and across the number \emph{k} of periods of
exposure to higher treatment doses. Accordingly,\emph{βlp,ℓ}does not
estimate an average across groups of the effect of \emph{ℓ}periods of
exposure to higher treatment doses:\emph{βlp,ℓ}is contaminated by
effects of other lengths of exposure. It is easy to understand where
this contamination issue stems from in binary and staggered designs.
Then, some groups with \emph{Dg,t} = 1 may have started receiving the
treatment before period \emph{t}, so the local-projection regression of
\emph{Yg,t−}1+\emph{ℓ}on \emph{Dg,t} captures an effect of more than
\emph{ℓ}periods of exposure for those groups. Similarly, some groups
with \emph{Dg,t} = 0, which are supposed to be control groups, may have
started receiving treatment between periods \emph{t}+1 and
\emph{t−}1+\emph{ℓ}, and the local-projection regression of
\emph{Yg,t−}1+\emph{ℓ}on \emph{Dg,t} captures an effect of less than
\emph{ℓ}periods of exposure for those groups. Beyond this contamination
phenomenon, a further issue is that some of the weights may be negative,
and we actually show that for \emph{ℓ≥}2, some weights are always
negative in Design 1. A last and perhaps even more concerning issue is
that in Design 1, for \emph{ℓ∈\{}2\emph{, ..., \uline{F}\}}, the weights
\emph{wlp,ℓg,t}sum to strictly less than one. This implies that even if
there is a, say, positive real number \emph{θ} such
that\emph{δg,k/}¯\emph{Dg,k} = \emph{θ}, meaning that the treatment
effect does not vary with length of exposure \emph{k} or across groups
\emph{g}, \emph{E} \emph{βlp,ℓ} \emph{\textless{} θ}: the
local-projection regression is downward biased. This is because the
regression is misspecified: it considers groups with \emph{Dg,t} = 0 as
untreated, whereas some of them may actually be treated at \emph{t −}1 +
\emph{ℓ}.

34

\textbf{Connection with other literature on local projection
regressions.} The issues with local-projection regressions we highlight
in Proposition 2 are specific to Regression 2, an extension of local
projection to panel data. Those issues are absent with time-series data
where the unit receives a single shock, the design considered by Jordà
(2005). Those issues are also absent in the careful extension of local
projection regressions to panel data with binary and staggered
treatments recently proposed by Dube et al. (2023), in work posterior to
ours.

\textbf{4.3} \textbf{Distributed-lag regressions.}

\textbf{Distributed-lag regressions.} Among the nine papers in our
census that do not have a binary and staggered design and estimate
dynamic effects, the third estimation method we found are
distributed-lag regressions.

\textbf{Regression 3} \emph{(Distributed-lag regression) For l
∈\{}0\emph{, ..., K\}, letβdl,l denote the coefficient on Dg,t−l in a
regression of Yg,t on group and period FEs and}
(\emph{Dg,t−l})\emph{l∈\{}0\emph{,...,K\}, in the sub-sample such that t
≥K} + 1\emph{.}

Regression 3 is discussed in Angrist and Pischke (2008), see Equation
(5.2.6) therein. In prac-tice, researchers may slightly augment or
modify Regression 3. They may include treatment leads in the regression,
to test for parallel trends. They may define the lagged treatments as
equal to 0 at time periods when they are not observed, and estimate the
regression in the full sample. They may also estimate the regression in
first difference and without group fixed effects. Results similar to
Proposition 3 below apply to all those variations on Regression 3.

\textbf{A decomposition of the coefficients in distributed-lag
regressions.}

\textbf{Proposition 3} \emph{(Application of Corollary 1 in de
Chaisemartin and D'Haultfœuille (2023\uline{a}) to Regression 3) If
Regression 3 is well-defined, Assumptions 1-2 hold and for all g and}

