meaning that the functional form of the distributed-lag regression is
correctly specified: only the first \emph{K} treatment lags affect the
outcome, and those lags do not interact. Even under those strong
assumptions, Proposition 3 shows that\emph{βdl,l}, the coefficient on
the \emph{l}th treatment lag, may not estimate a well-defined causal
effect. Specifically, Proposition 3 shows that this coefficient
estimates the sum of \emph{K} +1 terms. The first term is a weighted sum
of the effect of the \emph{l}th treatment lag, across all (\emph{g, t})
cells for which that lag is not equal to 0, with weights that sum to one
but may be negative. This term may be biased for the average effect of
the \emph{l}th treatment lag, if that effect varies across (\emph{g, t})
cells. The remaining \emph{K} terms are weighted sums of the effects of
other treatment lags, with weights summing to zero. If the effects of
the other lags vary across (\emph{g, t}) cells, those terms may differ
from zero and may contaminate

