\emph{βdl,l}.19

\textbf{5 Application to banking deregulations and the hous-}

\textbf{ing market}

\textbf{5.1} \textbf{Setting and research question}

In 1994, the Interstate Banking and Branching Efficiency Act (IBBEA)
allowed US Banks to operate across state borders without formal
authorization from state authorities. Initially, all states still
imposed restrictions on: de novo branching without explicit agreement by
state authorities; the minimum age of the target institution in case of
mergers; the acquisition of individual branches without acquiring the
entire bank; the total amount of statewide deposits controlled by a
single bank or bank holding company. Rice and Strahan (2010) compute the
number of restrictions in place in each state and year from 1994 to
2005. Favara and Imbs (2015) reverse their index, so their treatment is
the number of restrictions lifted, ranging from 0 to 4. Several papers
in the finance literature have aggregated these qualitatively different
deregulations into a scalar variable. As the treatment is equal to 0 in
every state in 1994, our event-study parameters are just average effects
of having experienced some deregulations versus none, and do not rely on
any homogeneity assumption on the effects of different policies.20Favara
and Imbs (2015) use 1994-to-2005 county-level data to estimate the
effect of the number of regulations lifted on the growth of mortgages
originated by banks, and on the growth of houses prices.

19 The twowayfeweights command can be used to compute the weights
attached to distributed-lag re-gressions, using the other\_treatments
option.

20Since no state had conducted deregulations prior to 1994, the
initial-conditions problem is also not an issue.
\end{quote}

37

\begin{quote}
\textbf{5.2} \textbf{Local-projection-regressions}

\textbf{Regression model.} Favara and Imbs (2015) use a panel data
version of the local-projection regressions proposed by Jordà (2005).
Let ∆denote the first-difference operator. To esti-mate the treatment
effect on, say, the growth rate of loan volume, they regress, for
every\emph{ℓ∈\{}1\emph{, ...,} 9\emph{\}}, ∆ln(\emph{Lg,t−}1+\emph{ℓ}),
the log growth rate of loans in county \emph{g} in year \emph{t −}1 +
\emph{ℓ}, on county and year FEs, \emph{Dg,t−}1, the number of
deregulations in county \emph{g} and year \emph{t −}1, and some
controls. Proposition 2 does not apply to their specification, because
the authors define their treatment variable as \emph{Dg,t−}1, the lagged
treatment, and because the regression has some controls. Thus, we
reestimate their local-projection regression, with \emph{Dg,t} instead
of \emph{Dg,t−}1 and without controls. The top-left panel of Figure 1
below shows that doing so, we find significant and positive
coefficients\emph{βlp,ℓ}till \emph{ℓ}= 5, insignificant coefficients at
\emph{ℓ}= 6, 7, and 9, and a significantly negative coefficient at
\emph{ℓ}= 8. This is in line with the results the authors obtained with
their regression, except that with their specification the coefficient
remains positive and significant till \emph{ℓ}= 4 and is not
significantly negative at \emph{ℓ}= 8 (see their Figure 1, Panel B).
These results lead the authors to conclude that the growth effects of
deregulation on credit supply are temporary. The bottom-left panel of
Figure 1 shows that results are similar for the log growth rate of
houses prices: coefficients are marginally significant and positive till
\emph{ℓ}= 4, and insignificant after.

\textbf{Decomposition of the local-projection coefficients.} We use
Proposition 2 to decom-pose the local-projection coefficients shown on
the top-left panel of Figure 1.21 \emph{βlp,}1 is a weighted sum of
7,626 effects \emph{δg,k/}¯\emph{Dg,k}, where 4,670 effects are weighted
positively and 2,956 effects are weighted negatively, and where positive
and negative weights respectively sum to 1\emph{.}067 and
\emph{−}0\emph{.}125. Weights do not sum to one, because this
application does not meet the conditions in Design 3, but their sum is
still not very far from one.\emph{βlp,}1, which is supposed to measure
the effect of one year of exposure to treatment, is contaminated by
effects of other 21Results are very similar though not completely
identical for the local-projection coefficients shown on the bottom-left
panel, because counties with missing values are not exactly the same for
the two outcomes.
\end{quote}

