\textbf{5.3} \textbf{DID}\emph{ℓ}\textbf{and
DID}\emph{nℓ}\textbf{estimators.}

\textbf{Details on the design.} Eight states never deregulate. 33 states
deregulate only once, eight states deregulate twice, and one state
deregulates three times. Of these 42 states that deregulate at least
once, 38 do so for the first time in 1995, 1996, 1997, or 1998.
Accordingly, up to \emph{ℓ}= 2005 \emph{−}1998 + 1 = 8, the
\emph{δℓ}parameters apply to similar sets of counties: \emph{δ}1
and\emph{δ}8 respectively apply to 905 and 773 counties. \emph{δ}9,
\emph{δ}10, and \emph{δ}11 on the other hand only apply to a smaller
subsample of counties (357, 238, and 1 county, respectively). Hence, we
do not report estimates of those parameters.22Similarly, five placebo
estimators can be computed, but only three apply to more than 50\% of
the 905 counties whose treatment changes at least once. The four and
fifth placebos only apply to 128 and 120 counties respectively, so we do
22For the two outcomes we consider, the estimates of \emph{δ}9 and
\emph{δ}10 are even larger than those of \emph{δ}8.
\end{quote}

39

\begin{quote}
not report them.23

\textbf{DID}\emph{ℓ}\textbf{estimators, for the loan-volume outcome.}
The top-center panel of Figure 1 plots the DID\emph{ℓ}estimators for the
loan-volume outcome to the right of zero, and the cor-responding placebo
estimators to the left. DID1 = 0\emph{.}043 (s.e.=0\emph{.}035): after
one year of exposure to deregulation, the loan-volume growth rate
increases by 4.3 percentage points more in counties that deregulate than
in counties that do not, an insignificant difference. This effect builds
up over time, and becomes significant at the 10\% level after three
years of exposure (DID3 = 0\emph{.}081, s.e.=0\emph{.}049), and at the
5\% level after five years of exposure (DID5 = 0\emph{.}148,
s.e.=0\emph{.}064). To the left of zero, placebo estimates are shown.
Placebos are jointly insignificant (F-test p-value=0\emph{.}400). But
the placebos are relatively large, and imprecisely estimated. For
instance, several DID\emph{ℓ}estimates are below the line connecting the
lower bound of the confidence interval of DIDpl 1and (0\emph{,} 0).
Moreover, we can only test the parallel-trends assumption over three
years, whereas that assumption has to hold for eight years for DID8 to
be unbiased. Overall, even if the placebos do not clearly indicate a
violation of the parallel-trends assumption, they may fail to detect
violations of parallel trends large enough to substantially bias the
DID\emph{ℓ}estimates (see Roth, 2022).

\textbf{DID}\emph{nℓ}\textbf{estimators, for the loan-volume outcome.}
The top-right panel of Figure 1 plots the DID\emph{nℓ}estimators for the
loan-volume outcome. In order to test for Point 1 of Lemma 7, we
restrict the sample of switchers to the 773 counties for which
DID\emph{g,}8 can be computed, and further discard 141 switchers in
states that experienced more than one treatment change (results are not
very different if one does not restrict the sample). DID\emph{n}\\
\emph{ℓ}are estimators of the effect of the current treatment and of its
\emph{ℓ−}1 first lags on the outcome. In this restricted sample, each
lag receives a weight equal to 1\emph{/ℓ}. With the exception of
DID\emph{n} 1, which is much larger than the other estimates but also
imprecisely estimated, all estimates are relatively close to each other,
suggesting that treatment effects are relatively stable over time, and
that 23Numbers are for the volume-of-mortgages-originated outcome;
numbers would slightly differ for the houses-price outcome: counties
with missing values are not exactly the same for the two outcomes.
\end{quote}

40

the effects of the current and lagged treatments are not very different.
To formally test this, we follow Point 1 of Lemma 7 in the Web Appendix,
and test whether \emph{ℓ→}DID\emph{nℓ}is constant. The test is not
rejected (p-value = 0.328), which goes against the authors' conclusion
that deregulations only have short-lived effects on mortgage volume. Our
decompositions of their local projection estimators suggest that their
coefficients may become small and insignificant because the sum of the
weights attached to\emph{βlp,ℓ}decreases as \emph{ℓ}increases.

\textbf{DID}\emph{ℓ}\textbf{and DID}\emph{nℓ}\textbf{estimators, for the
houses-prices outcome.} The bottom-center panel of Figure 1 plots the
DID\emph{ℓ}estimators for the houses-prices outcome. DID1 = 0\emph{.}003
(s.e.=0\emph{.}004): after one year of exposure to deregulation, the
houses-price growth rate in-creases by 0.3 percentage points more in
counties that deregulate than in counties that do not, an insignificant
difference. This effect builds up over time, and becomes significant at
the 10\% level after four years of exposure (DID4 = 0\emph{.}016,
s.e.=0\emph{.}009), and at the 5\% level after five years of exposure
(DID5 = 0\emph{.}026, s.e.=0\emph{.}010). Placebos are jointly
insignificant (F-test p-value=0\emph{.}703). For this outcome, the
placebos are small, fairly precisely estimated, and suggest that if
anything, differential pre-trends would downward-bias the estimated
ef-fects. The bottom-right panel of Figure 1 plots the
DID\emph{nℓ}estimators, restricting the sample of switchers to counties
for which DID\emph{g,}8 can be computed and in states that experienced
only one treatment change. Estimates increase with \emph{ℓ}:
deregulations seem to also have long-lasting effects on houses prices.

