\multicolumn{2}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 2\tabcolsep) * \real{1.0000} + 2\tabcolsep}@{}}{%
\begin{minipage}[t]{\linewidth}\raggedright
We now propose placebo estimators of Assumptions 1 and 2. First, for any
\emph{g} such that 3 \emph{≤}

\emph{Fg ≤Tg} and for any \emph{ℓ∈\{}1\emph{, ...,} min(\emph{Tg −Fg} +
1\emph{, Fg −}2)\emph{\}}, let

\begin{quote}
DIDpl \emph{g,ℓ}= \emph{Yg,Fg−}1\emph{−ℓ−Yg,Fg−}1 \emph{−Ng
Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ}1

\emph{g′}:\emph{Dg′,}1=\emph{Dg,}1\emph{,Fg′\textgreater Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ}\\
(\emph{Yg′,Fg−}1\emph{−ℓ−Yg′,Fg−}1)\emph{.}
\end{quote}

DIDpl \emph{g,ℓ}is a placebo estimator mimicking DID\emph{g,ℓ}. Like
DID\emph{g,ℓ}, it compares group \emph{g}'s outcome

evolution to that of groups with the same baseline treatment, and that
have kept that treatment

from period 1 to \emph{Fg −}1 + \emph{ℓ}. But unlike DID\emph{g,ℓ}, it
compares those groups' outcome evolutions

from period \emph{Fg −ℓ−}1 to period \emph{Fg −}1, namely before group
\emph{g}'s treatment changes for the first time. Accordingly, DIDpl
\emph{g,ℓ}assesses if \emph{g} and its control groups experience
parallel evolutions

of their status-quo potential outcomes, for \emph{ℓ}periods, the number
of periods over which parallel

trends has to hold for DID\emph{g,ℓ}to be unbiased. Note that while
DID\emph{g,ℓ}goes from the past (period

\emph{Fg −}1) to the future (period \emph{Fg −}1 + \emph{ℓ}), DIDpl
\emph{g,ℓ}goes from the future (period \emph{Fg −}1) to the

past (period \emph{Fg −}1 \emph{−ℓ}). This follows the standard practice
in event-study regressions, where the reference period is the one before
the event.

Then, let \emph{L}pl= max\emph{g\{}min(\emph{Tg −Fg} + 1\emph{, Fg
−}2)\emph{\}}. \emph{L}plis the largest \emph{ℓ}such that there is at
least

one group for which DID\emph{g,ℓ}can be estimated (\emph{Fg −}1 +
\emph{ℓ≤Tg}) and for which DIDpl \emph{g,ℓ}can also

be estimated (\emph{Fg −}1 \emph{−ℓ≥}1 \emph{⇔ℓ≤Fg −}2). One may have
\emph{L}pl= \emph{−}1, if all groups that switch treatment do so for the
first time at period 2. Then, none of the placebos defined below
can\strut
