\emph{g,ℓ}can be computed. Then, let

DIDpl\emph{ℓ}= \emph{N}pl 1

\begin{quote}
\emph{ℓ} \emph{g}:1\emph{≤Fg−}1\emph{−ℓ,Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ≤Tg}\\
\emph{Sg}DIDpl \emph{g,ℓ.}
\end{quote}

\textbf{Lemma 4} \emph{If Assumptions 1 and 2 hold and Lpl≥}1\emph{,
then ∀ℓ∈\{}1\emph{, ..., Lpl\}, E} \emph{DIDplℓ\textbar{}\textbf{D }} =
0\emph{.}

Lemma 4 shows that \emph{E} DIDpl\emph{ℓ\textbar{}\textbf{D }} = 0 is a
testable implication of Assumptions 1 and 2.

The placebo estimators DIDpl\emph{ℓ}mimick our actual estimators, but
accordingly they do not exhaust

all our assumptions' testable implications. There are at least two
advantages to having placebos

mimicking the actual estimators. First, when we reject \emph{E}
DIDpl\emph{ℓ\textbar{}\textbf{D }}= 0, the value of DIDpl\emph{ℓ}

