\emph{−Yg,Fg−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}
\emph{−E} \emph{Yg′,Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ}(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg′,Fg−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}
\end{quote}

does not depend on (\emph{g, g′}). In words, the sign of the difference
in trends between a switcher and one of its control group has to be the
same, for every pair of switcher and control. Then, the sign of the bias
of DID\emph{ℓ}is equal to the sign of
\emph{−E}{[}DIDpl\emph{ℓ\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}. The second, related
advantage of having placebo estimators mimicking the actual estimators
is that it makes them more amenable to the sensitivity analysis proposed
by Rambachan and Roth (2019), which allows researchers to assess how
sensitive their results are to violations of parallel trends. This
approach assumes that trends are not exactly parallel, and that one has
placebo estimators whose magnitude is informative of the magnitude of
switchers and controls differential trends after switchers' treatment
changes.

This requirement is more plausible when placebos closely mimick the
actual estimators.

\textbf{Computation.} Our placebo estimators are computed by the
did\_multiplegt\_dyn Stata package, when the placebo option is
specified.

\textbf{1.2} \textbf{Controlling for covariates}

\textbf{Identifying assumption.} To control for covariates, we replace
Assumption 2 by the following assumption. Let \emph{\textbf{X}g} =
(\emph{X′g,}1\emph{, ..., X′g,T}) and \emph{\textbf{X}} =
(\emph{\textbf{X}}1\emph{, ..., \textbf{X}G}).

\textbf{Assumption 7} \emph{(Parallel trends with covariates) There are
vectors} (\emph{θd})\emph{d∈Dr} 1\emph{of same dimension as Xg,t such
that ∀}(\emph{g, g′}) \emph{such that Dg,}1 = \emph{Dg′,}1 \emph{∈Dr}
1\emph{, then ∀t ≥}2\emph{,}

\begin{quote}
\emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t−}1) \emph{−}(\emph{Xg,t
−Xg,t−}1)\emph{′θDg,}1\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}, \textbf{X}}{]}
=\emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg′,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg′,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t−}1) \emph{−}(\emph{Xg′,t
−Xg′,t−}1)\emph{′θDg′,}1\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D},
\textbf{X}}{]}\emph{,}
\end{quote}

Assumption 7 is similar to Assumption 2, except that now groups can
experience differen-tial trends, provided those differential trends are
fully explained by changes in their covari-ates. Note that the effect of
these covariates is allowed to vary with groups' baseline-treatment
value. To control for time-invariant covariates, one can for instance
let \emph{Xg,t} = \emph{Xg × t}. Then, (\emph{Xg,t
−Xg,t−}1)\emph{′θDg,}1 = \emph{X′}sumption, with a linear time-invariant
functional form for the conditional counterfactual trend. \emph{gθDg,}1,
so Assumption 7 amounts to a conditional parallel-trends as-

Alternatively, one can let \emph{Xg,t} be a vector of interactions
between \emph{Xg} and (1\emph{\{t ≥t′\}})\emph{t′∈\{}1\emph{,...,T\}},
in-dicators for period \emph{t} being after \emph{t′}. Then, Assumption
7 amounts to a conditional parallel-trends assumption, with a linear
period-specific functional form for the conditional counterfactual
trend.

