\textbf{1.4} \textbf{Allowing for different trends across sets of
groups}

\textbf{Identifying assumption.} In some cases, controlling for
covariates may be insufficient to account for differences in trends
between groups. Then, a common remedy in static or dynamic two-way fixed
effect regressions consists in including interactions between time FE
and FE for sets of groups. For instance, if groups are US counties, one
can allow for state-specific trends. A similar idea can be pursued in
our context. Let \emph{s}(\emph{g}) \emph{∈\{}1\emph{, ..., S\}} denote
the set of groups \emph{g} belongs to. In this set-up, we modify our
assumptions as follows.

\textbf{Assumption 9} \emph{(Parallel trends within sets of groups)
∀}(\emph{g, g′})\emph{, if s}(\emph{g}) = \emph{s}(\emph{g′})\emph{,
Dg,}1 = \emph{Dg′,}1 \emph{∈Dr} 1\emph{, then ∀t ≥}2\emph{,}\\
\emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}
= \emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg′,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg′,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}\emph{.}

6

Assumption 9 is a weakening of Assumption 2, as it only requires that
the status-quo potential outcome of groups in the same set of groups
follow the same evolution over time.

\textbf{Design Restriction 4} \emph{(Restriction on the design, with
sets of groups) ∃}(\emph{g, g′}) : \emph{s}(\emph{g}) =
\emph{s}(\emph{g′})\emph{, Dg,}1 = \emph{Dg′,}1 \emph{and Fg \textless{}
Fg′.}

Restriction 4 is a strengthening of Restriction 1: we should now observe
some variation in the timing of the first switch not only among groups
with the same baseline treatment, but also within the same set of
groups.

