when the trends\_nonparam option is specified.

\textbf{1.5} \textbf{Estimating and testing the heterogeneity of
treatment effects.}

Let \emph{βhetℓ} denote the coefficients on \emph{Xg} in an (unfeasible)
OLS regression of \emph{Sgδg,ℓ}, the effect of having been exposed to a
weakly higher treatment for \emph{ℓ}periods in group \emph{g} on
\emph{Xg} and indicators for all possible values of \emph{Fg ×Dg,}1
\emph{×Sg}, in the sample of groups such that \emph{Fg −}1+\emph{ℓ≤Tg}.
\emph{βhet} a relevant quantity to test for heterogeneous treatment
effects. If \emph{Xg} is a scalar binary variable, is

it follows from Angrist (1998) that \emph{βhetℓ} is a variance-weighted
average of comparisons of \emph{Sgδg,ℓ}across groups with \emph{Xg} = 1
and \emph{Xg} = 0 belonging to the same \emph{Fg ×Dg,}1 \emph{×Sg} cell.
It follows from

7

Angrist and Krueger (1999) that a similar interpretation holds whenever
\emph{Xg} is a scalar ordered variable: for instance, if \emph{Xg} is
continuous, \emph{βhet} is a variance-weighted average of the derivative
of the conditional mean of \emph{Sgδg,ℓ}with respect to \emph{Xg},
within \emph{Fg × Dg,}1 \emph{× Sg} cells. When \emph{Xg} is a vector,
\emph{βhetℓ} may no longer be interpreted as an average of average
differences of \emph{Sgδg,ℓ}across groups with different values of
\emph{Xg} and the same value of \emph{Fg × Dg,}1 \emph{× Sg}. Still, it
can be used to test the null that \emph{Sgδg,ℓ}does not vary across
\emph{g}, and to identify covariates that predict \emph{Sgδg,ℓ}.

To estimate \emph{βhetℓ} , we consider\emph{βhetℓ}, the coefficients on
\emph{Xg} in OLS regressions of
\emph{Sg}(\emph{Yg,Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ−Yg,Fg−}1) on \emph{Xg} and indicators
for all possible values of \emph{Fg × Dg,}1 \emph{× Sg}, in the sample
of groups such that \emph{Fg −}1 + \emph{ℓ≤Tg}. We thus use
\emph{Sg}(\emph{Yg,Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ−Yg,Fg−}1) rather than
\emph{Sg}DID\emph{g,ℓ}as the regression outcome. The advantage is that
inference does not need to account for the fact the DID\emph{g,ℓ}s are
estimated. Lemma 6 shows that\emph{βhetℓ} is unbiased for \emph{βhetℓ} ,
under the following parallel trends assumption.

\textbf{Assumption 10} \emph{(Parallel trends to test for heterogeneous
effects) ∀}(\emph{g, g′}) \emph{such that Dg,}1 = \emph{Dg′,}1
\emph{∈Dr} 1\emph{, ∀t ≥}2\emph{,}

\emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D},
\textbf{X}}{]} =\emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg′,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t})
\emph{−Yg′,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g′,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D},
\textbf{X}}{]}\emph{.}

\textbf{Lemma 6} \emph{If Assumptions 1 and 10 hold, then ∀ℓ∈\{}1\emph{,
..., L\}, E} \emph{βhetℓ} \emph{\textbf{D}, \textbf{X }} = \emph{βhetℓ}
\emph{.}

\emph{βhetℓ} does not make use of control groups. That such groups are
not necessary to estimate heterogeneous treatment effects in DID studies
was already noted before this paper by Shahn (2023) and de Chaisemartin
and D'Haultfœuille (2023), in simple designs without variation in
treatment intensity or timing. Lemma 6 shows that their results extend
to more general designs, provided one controls for indicators for all
possible values of \emph{Fg × Dg,}1 \emph{× Sg}. Our procedure cannot be
used to estimate if \emph{δn g,ℓ}varies across values of \emph{Xg}, but
one can control for \emph{δD g,ℓ}in the heterogeneity analysis, to
assess if \emph{Xg} predicts \emph{Sgδg,ℓ}even controlling for \emph{δD
g,ℓ}.

\textbf{1.6} \textbf{Separately identifying switching-in and
switching-out effects in Design 2}

Consider Design 2, in which \emph{Dg,t} = 1\emph{\{Eg ≥t ≥Fg\}}, with
the convention that \emph{Eg} = \emph{T} + 1 if \emph{Fg ≤T} and
\emph{g} never leaves treatment. Then, we show that one can separately
estimate the effects of switching in and switching out of the treatment.
First, let \emph{N}+ let \emph{ℓ}= \#\emph{\{g} : \emph{Eg −Fg
≥ℓ−}1\emph{\}} and

\emph{δ}+\emph{ℓ}= \emph{N}+ 1

\begin{quote}
\emph{ℓ} \emph{g}:\emph{Eg−Fg≥ℓ−}1\\
\emph{δg,ℓ.} (7)
\end{quote}

This parameter is the average effect of having been treated for
\emph{ℓ}periods, across all groups that have not switched out of the
treatment \emph{ℓ}periods after the first date where they go treated. If
there exist (\emph{g, g′}) such that \emph{ℓ≤Eg −Fg} + 1 and \emph{Fg′
≥Eg −Fg} + 1, we can unbiasedly estimate

8

\emph{δ}+\emph{ℓ}under Assumptions 1-2, by replacing \emph{δg,ℓ}by
DID\emph{g,ℓ}in (7). In practice, this estimator can be

computed using the did\_multiplegt\_dyn command, on the subsample of
(\emph{g, t})s such that \emph{g} has

not switched out of the treatment at or before \emph{t}.

Next, let us define

\emph{δ−g,ℓ}=
\emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg,Eg}+\emph{ℓ}(\textbf{0}\emph{Fg−}1\emph{,}
\textbf{1}\emph{Eg−Fg}+1\emph{,} \textbf{0}\emph{ℓ})
\emph{−Yg,Eg}+\emph{ℓ}(\textbf{0}\emph{Fg−}1\emph{,}
\textbf{1}\emph{Eg−Fg}+1+\emph{ℓ})\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}\emph{.}

\emph{δ−g,ℓ}is the effect of having been untreated rather than treated
for \emph{ℓ}periods, in group \emph{g} and

at period \emph{Eg} + \emph{ℓ}. To unbiasedly estimate \emph{δ−g,ℓ}, we
consider the following modified version of

Assumption 2:

\textbf{Assumption 11} \emph{(Parallel trends if groups treated at the
same date never leave treatment)}

\emph{∀}(\emph{g, g′})\emph{, if Fg} = \emph{Fg′, then ∀t ≥Fg,}

\emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg,t}(\textbf{0}\emph{Fg−}1\emph{,}
\textbf{1}\emph{t−}(\emph{Fg−}1))
\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\textbf{0}\emph{Fg−}1\emph{,}
\textbf{1}\emph{t−}1\emph{−}(\emph{Fg−}1))\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}

=\emph{E}{[}\emph{Yg′,t}(\textbf{0}\emph{Fg−}1\emph{,}
\textbf{1}\emph{t−}(\emph{Fg−}1))
\emph{−Yg′,t−}1(\textbf{0}\emph{Fg−}1\emph{,}
\textbf{1}\emph{t−}1\emph{−}(\emph{Fg−}1))\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}\emph{.}

Now, let \emph{Ng,−ℓ} = \#\emph{\{Eg} + \emph{ℓ≤T, ∃g′}: \emph{Fg′} =
\emph{Fg, Eg′ ≥Eg} + \emph{ℓ\}} and assume that
\emph{Ng,−\textgreater{}} 0. Then,

under Assumptions 1 and 11,

\begin{quote}
DID\emph{−g,ℓ}= \emph{Yg,Eg}+\emph{ℓ−Yg,Eg−Ng,−}1

\emph{ℓ} \emph{g′}:\emph{Fg′}=\emph{Fg,Eg′≥Eg}+\emph{ℓ}
\emph{Yg′,Eg}+\emph{ℓ−Yg′,Eg}
\end{quote}

is an unbiased estimator of \emph{δ−g,ℓ}.

Finally, let \emph{N−ℓ}= \#\emph{\{g} : \emph{N g,−\textgreater{}}
0\emph{\}} and define the aggregated effect

1

\begin{quote}
\emph{δ−ℓ}= \emph{N−ℓ} \emph{g}:\emph{Ng,−ℓ} \emph{\textgreater{}}0
\emph{δ−g,ℓ.}
\end{quote}

We can unbiasedly estimate \emph{δ−ℓ}by replacing \emph{δ−g,ℓ}by
DID\emph{−g,ℓ}in the previous display.

\textbf{Computation.} \emph{δ−ℓ}can be estimated using the
did\_multiplegt\_dyn command on the sub-

sample of (\emph{g, t})s such that group \emph{g} has switched from 0 to
1 at or before \emph{t}, including the date

of that switch in the trends\_nonparam option, and defining the
treatment as an indicator for

switching from 1 to 0. Assumptions 1 and 11 can be tested by adding the
placebo option to

that estimation.

\textbf{1.7} \textbf{Fuzzy designs}

In this subsection, we briefly discuss fuzzy designs. These correspond
to cases with \emph{Ng,t \textgreater{}} 1 units

in group \emph{g} at \emph{t}, and where treatment varies across units
within (\emph{g, t}) cells. The estimators we

9

propose in this paper are readily applicable if all groups are fully
untreated at period 1. Then, one just needs to redefine the treatment,
the counterfactual outcome without any treatment, and the observed
treatment of cell (\emph{g, t}) as follows. Let \emph{Di,g,t} denote the
treatment of unit \emph{i} in cell (\emph{g, t}), and let \emph{Dg,t} =
1\emph{/Ng,t} \emph{i}=1\emph{Di,g,t}denote the average treatment in
cell (\emph{g, t}). Also, let \emph{Yi,g,t}(\textbf{0}\emph{t}) denote
the potential outcome of unit \emph{i} in cell (\emph{g, t}) without any
treatment from period 1 to \emph{t}, and let \emph{Yi,g,t} denote her
observed outcome. Then, we let \emph{Yg,t}(\textbf{0}\emph{t}) =
1\emph{/Ng,t} \emph{i}=1\emph{Yi,g,t}(\textbf{0}\emph{t}) and
\emph{Yg,t} = 1\emph{/Ng,t} \emph{i}=1\emph{Yi,g,t}. With this notation,
we can show that the estimators defined in Section 3 are conditionally
unbiased for the causal effects defined therein, under Assumptions 1 and
2. For instance, the (DID\emph{ℓ})1\emph{≤ℓ≤L} are conditionally
unbiased for the (\emph{δℓ})1\emph{≤ℓ≤L}. Thus, our estimators can still
be used, even if groups' potential outcomes depend on the identities of
treated units.

In groups partly treated at period 1, the estimators proposed in this
paper are not applicable. To simplify the discussion, let us momentarily
assume that the individual-level treatment is binary. Then, only groups
where the proportion of treated units does not change over time can be
used as controls, and such groups may not exist. When such groups exist,
two estimation strategies, which differ from the ones considered in this
paper, are available. First, one may separately estimate the outcome
evolution of treated and untreated units in the control groups, and
apply those counterfactual evolutions separately to treated and control
units in groups whose pro-portion of treated units does change, and thus
recover the counterfactual outcome those groups would have experienced
if their proportion of treated units had not changed (see de
Chaisemartin and D'Haultfœuille, 2018). Second, one may redefine the
treatment as a continuous, group-level variable, and apply the
estimators proposed by de Chaisemartin et al. (2022).

\textbf{1.8} \textbf{Ruling out lagged treatment effects, and
sensitivity to initial conditions}

Up to now, we have made no restriction on the effects of past
treatments. We now investigate the benefits of imposing such
restrictions. Specifically, we consider the following assumption.

\textbf{Assumption 12\emph{-}}\emph{k (No effect of past treatments
beyond k lags)}

\emph{For all} (\emph{g, t}) \emph{and all} (\emph{d}1\emph{, ..., dt})
\emph{in the support of} (\emph{D}1\emph{, ..., Dt})\emph{,
Yg,t}(\emph{d}1\emph{, ..., dt}) = \emph{Yg,t}(\emph{dt−k, ...,
dt})\emph{.}

Assumption 12-\emph{k} is plausible when the treatment is unlikely to
have very long-run effects. It is commonly made in event-study
regressions and extensively discussed in Borusyak et al. (2023) and in
Schmidheiny and Siegloch (2020) as a possible way to identify these
regressions.

In our context, imposing Assumption 12-\emph{k} can provide a solution
to the ``initial conditions''problem. So far, we have assumed that the
treatments prior to the start of the panel (\emph{Dg,t})\emph{t≤}0 do
not affect potential outcomes. There are at least two situations where
this assumption is innocuous. First, such treatments may not exist.
Assume for instance that one seeks to

10

estimate the effect of being unionized on earnings, using the NLSY
panel. In this data set, \emph{t} = 1 corresponds to the first year on
the labor market, so \emph{Dg,t} is not defined for \emph{t ≤}0. Second,
in Designs 1-3, our main results still hold if potential outcomes depend
on (\emph{Dg,t})\emph{t≤}0. In groups untreated at period 1, \emph{Dg,t}
= 0 for all \emph{t ≤}0, so our results still hold. Outside of those
designs, our results do not apply when potential outcomes depend on
(\emph{Dg,t})\emph{t≤}0. However, under Assumption 12-\emph{k}, our
results apply to a restricted panel, including groups with a stable
treatment from period 1 to \emph{k} +1, and starting at period \emph{k}
+1. Note that a similar idea was already put forward by Schmidheiny and
Siegloch (2020) in the context of event-study regressions, except that
in the context of those regressions one only needs to drop the first
\emph{k} + 1 periods of the panel, while we also need to drop groups
whose treatment changes at some point between periods 1 and \emph{k} +
1.

Computing our estimators on the aforementioned restricted panel may
yield very noisy esti-mators, however. An alternative to suggestively
assess if one's results are sensitive to initial conditions is to
recompute the normalized estimators proposed in the paper, after
dropping, say, all time periods up to \emph{F−}1
\emph{Fg\textbar Fg≤T}(0\emph{.}2), where \emph{F −}1
\emph{Fg\textbar Fg≤T}(\emph{α}) is the quantile of order \emph{α} of
\emph{Fg} among all groups experiencing at least one treatment change.
Finding similar estimators with and without dropping those estimators
may be reassuring, though this is of course not a formal test.

\textbf{1.9} \textbf{Using normalized event-study effects to test the
null that the current and} \textbf{lagged treatments have the same
effects.}

