\textbf{Lemma 7} \emph{Suppose that groups' treatment only changes once:
Dg,t} = \emph{Dg,Fg for all t ≥Fg. Suppose also that for all
ℓ∈\{}1\emph{, ..., ℓ′\} and all k ∈\{}0\emph{, . . . , ℓ−}1\emph{\},
sg,ℓ,k} = \emph{sg,k for some sg,k.}

\emph{Then:}

\begin{quote}
\emph{1. If sg,}0 = \emph{· · ·} = \emph{sg,ℓ′−}1\emph{, δn,bal} =
\emph{· · ·} = \emph{δn,balℓ′,ℓ′ .}

\emph{2. If sg,}0 \emph{≥· · · ≥sg,ℓ′−}1 \emph{≥}0\emph{, δn,bal≥· · ·
≥δn,balℓ′,ℓ′ .}

\emph{3. If sg,}0 \emph{≤· · · ≤sg,ℓ′−}1 \emph{≤}0\emph{, δn,bal≤· · ·
≤δn,balℓ′,ℓ′ .}
\end{quote}

Point 1 of Lemma 7 assumes that \emph{sg,ℓ,k} = \emph{sg,k} = \emph{sg},
meaning that the slope of the expected potential outcome function of
group \emph{g} at \emph{Fg −}1 + \emph{ℓ}with respect to its \emph{k}th
treatment lag does not depend on \emph{ℓ}or \emph{k}: the current and
lagged treatments have the same effects on the outcome,

11

and those effects do not change over time. Testing whether the current
and lagged treatments have the same effect on the outcome is often of
great economic interest, for instance to assess if treatment effects
tend to disappear as time since exposure increases. Point 1 of Lemma 7
provides a joint test of this null hypothesis, and of the null
hypothesis that treatment effects do not change over time: together,
these two hypotheses imply that \emph{ℓ→δn,bal} is constant. Similarly,
Point 2 of Lemma 7 provides a joint test of the null hypothesis that the
effect of lagged treatments are positive but decreasing with the number
of lags, and of the null hypothesis that treatment effects do not change
over time: together, these two hypotheses imply that \emph{ℓ→δn,bal} is
decreasing. Finally, Point 3 of Lemma 7 provides a joint test of the
null hypothesis that the effect of lagged treatments are negative but
increasing with the number of lags, and of the null hypothesis that
treatment effects do not change over time: together, these two
hypotheses imply that \emph{ℓ→δn,bal} is increasing. We can test those
testable implications using estimators DID\emph{n,balℓ′,ℓ′} of
\emph{δn,balℓ,ℓ′} , through a joint equality test for the first testable
implication, and a multivariate inequality test for the second and third
ones (see, e.g., Cox and Shi, 2023, for a recent and simple proposal).
Lemma 7 assumes that groups' treatment changes only once. A similar
result holds in more general designs, if
\emph{ℓ→\textbar Dg,Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ−Dg,}1\emph{\textbar{}} is decreasing
in \emph{ℓ}for \emph{ℓ≥}1, meaning that groups' treatment can change
multiple times, but tends to revert to its baseline value after the
initial treatment change, as is for instance the case in Design 2. Then,
the testable implications, instead of involving ``balanced'' versions of
\emph{δnℓ}, involve ``balanced'' versions of unweighted averages of the

\emph{δn g,ℓ}parameters.

\textbf{Computation.} The test based on Point 1 of Lemma 7 is computed
by the did\_multiplegt\_dyn Stata package, when the effects\_equal
option is specified. The same\_switchers option should also be
specified, to ensure event-study effects apply to the same set of
groups, and the normalized option needs to be specified as well.

\textbf{1.10} \textbf{Estimators when the period-one treatment is
continuous.}

\textbf{Identifying assumption.} To handle the case where groups'
period-one treatment is contin-uous, thus implying that i) in
Restriction 1 fails, we replace Assumption 2, our parallel trends
assumption on the status-quo outcome, by the following condition. Let
\emph{D}1 denote the support of groups' period-one treatment.

\textbf{Assumption 13} \emph{(Functional parallel trends for the
status-quo outcome, conditional on the period-one treatment) For all t
≥}2\emph{, ∃ϕt} : \emph{D}1 \emph{→}R \emph{such that
E}{[}\emph{Yg,t}(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t})\emph{−Yg,t−}1(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t−}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}{]}
=\emph{ϕt}(\emph{Dg,}1)\emph{.}

Assumption 13 is a mild strengthening of Assumption 2.

12

\textbf{Estimation.} We redefine \emph{Tg} = max\emph{g′ Fg′ −}1 and
otherwise keep the same definition of our

target parameters as in the paper. Under Assumptions 1 and 13,

\begin{quote}
\emph{δg,ℓ}= \emph{E}\emph{Yg,Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ−Yg,Fg−}1
\emph{−Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ}

\emph{t}=\emph{Fgϕt}(\emph{Dg,}1)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}\emph{.}
\end{quote}

Assume that one can unbiasedly estimate \emph{ϕt}(\emph{Dg,}1)
conditional on the design, and
let\emph{ϕt}(\emph{\textbf{D}g,}1\emph{,t})

denote the corresponding estimator. Then,

\emph{Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ}

\begin{quote}
\emph{Yg,Fg−}1+\emph{ℓ−Yg,Fg−}1 \emph{−} \emph{t}=\emph{Fg}
\emph{ϕt}(\emph{Dg,}1)
\end{quote}

is conditionally unbiased for \emph{δg,ℓ}. \emph{ϕt}(\emph{Dg,}1) can be
unbiasedly estimated conditional on the design,

under a functional-form assumption. For instance, if one assumes that

\emph{K}

\begin{quote}
\emph{ϕt}(\emph{Dg,}1) =\emph{γkfk}(\emph{Dg,}1)\emph{,} (8)
\end{quote}

\emph{k}=0

for some known functions (\emph{f}0\emph{, ..., fK}) (e.g.:
\emph{fk}(\emph{Dg,}1) = \emph{Dk g,}1), \emph{ϕt}(\emph{Dg,}1) can be
unbiasedly es-

timated by regressing \emph{Yg,t −Yg,t−}1 on the
(\emph{fk}(\emph{Dg,}1))\emph{k}=0\emph{,...,K}, in the sample of groups
such that \emph{Fg \textgreater{} t}. Importantly, the estimator we
propose is parametric and relies on the researcher's choice

of a functional form for \emph{ϕt}(\emph{Dg,}1). We also do not derive
the estimator's asymptotic distribution,

and rely on the bootstrap for inference. Proposing a non-parametric
estimator could be achieved

leveraging ideas from de Chaisemartin et al. (2022), but we leave this
extension for future work.

